From 317b96a1ac9fbc86f6a43bd602fb8757f30e78f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 10:42:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] WebCore: the window painted through a GraphicsContext on
 OpenGPU

GraphicsContextOpenGPU (platform/graphics/amiga) is the window's context
on the Amiga. Solid rectangles, clearRect, rectangle clips and pictures go
into one opengpu.library batch per paint; every other call goes to a
GraphicsContextCairo on the same pixels, which gets every state change as
it happens, after the batch is sent and drawn. The calls cairo draws are
counted by kind for the browser's OBVIEW_BENCH line.

OpenGPU draws, as cairo would:
- fillRect with a solid colour (the global alpha as cairo applies it) and
  clearRect, under a translate or scale, where the edges the clip leaves
  visible fall on whole pixels: FILL for copy, clear and opaque
  source-over, COMPOSITE_PD with a solid source for translucent
  source-over, source-atop, destination-over, destination-out, xor and
  plus-lighter;
- clips to rectangles on whole pixels, nested, with save and restore;
- drawNativeImage of a cairo ARGB32 or RGB24 image surface, with the
  global alpha, by the same operators but clear: pixel for pixel through
  COMPOSITE_PD from a slot; scaled or turned (nearest for
  DoNotInterpolate, bilinear for Low), a PATH_FILL of its rectangle
  painted with it as a PAD pattern, premultiplied as cairo is.
cairo keeps source-in, source-out, destination-in, destination-atop,
plus-darker, difference and the blend modes, gradients, patterns, paths,
strokes, rounded rectangles, glyphs, text lines, focus rings, shadows,
transparency layers, clipToImageBuffer, other clips, fractional edges,
pictures under GOOD or BEST filtering that are not drawn pixel for pixel,
and callers of platformContext().

The batch goes before any call cairo draws (consecutive cairo calls cost
one), when the stream or its scratch words are full, after drawing an
ImageBuffer (its pixels may change), and at the end; the cairo target is
flushed before it and marked dirty after it, and the pictures in it are
held until then. A failed batch leaves the rest of the paint to cairo.

OpenGPUPaintAmiga.c turns the state and the calls into stream words:
plain C, no WebKit, no floating point, tested on the host against
openamigartg's CPU core (openamigabrowser tests/test_opengpu_paint.c).
OpenGPULibraryAmiga.c opens opengpu.library once at run time through
openamigartg's inline calls; nothing more is linked. Without it, with a
library that answers OGPU_NONE for COMPOSITE_PD, or with the Shell
variable OB_PAINT=cairo, the browser paints with cairo as before.
PlatformAmiga.cmake lists the new files; the browser's build gives the
two C files openamigartg's headers.

